= Solution 2: File Operations
<solution-2-file-operations>
== Task 1: Merge Multiple Files
<task-1-merge-multiple-files>
=== Command:
<command>
```bash
cdo mergetime ../datasets/tas_2000.nc ../datasets/tas_2001.nc ../datasets/tas_2002.nc \
              ../datasets/tas_2003.nc ../datasets/tas_2004.nc ../datasets/tas_2005.nc \
              merged.nc
```

Or with a wildcard:

```bash
cdo mergetime ../datasets/tas_2???.nc merged.nc
```

=== Answers:
<answers>
+ #strong[Number of time steps:] 72 (6 years × 12 months)
+ #strong[Time range:] 2000-01-01 to 2005-12-01
+ #strong[Difference between `mergetime` and `merge`:]
  - `mergetime` concatenates files along the time dimension (assumes
    same variables/grid)
  - `merge` combines files with #strong[different variables] (joins
    along the variable/field dimension)
  - Use `mergetime` for time-slices, `merge` for different variables on
    the same time axis
+ #strong[Does `cdo copy` change file size?] Generally no (unless used
  with compression flags like `-z zip`). `copy` just copies the data
  with the same encoding.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

== Task 2: Split a File by Year and Month
<task-2-split-a-file-by-year-and-month>
=== Commands:
<commands>
```bash
cdo splityear ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_year_
cdo splitmon ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_mon_
cdo splitsel,1 ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_step_
```

=== Answers:
<answers-1>
+ #strong[splityear produces:] 31 files (one per year, 1980--2010). Each
  contains 12 monthly time steps.
+ #strong[splitmon produces:] 12 files (one per month). Each contains 31
  time steps (one for each year).
+ #strong[Naming patterns:]
  - `splityear`: `tas_year_1980.nc`, `tas_year_1981.nc`, …
  - `splitmon`: `tas_mon_01.nc`, `tas_mon_02.nc`, … (01 = January)
  - `splitsel`: `tas_step_001.nc`, `tas_step_002.nc`, …
+ #strong[Split by season:] Use `splitseas`. Produces 4 files per year
  (DJF, MAM, JJA, SON).

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

== Task 3: Select and Drop Variables
<task-3-select-and-drop-variables>
=== Commands:
<commands-1>
```bash
cdo selname,tas ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_only.nc
cdo delname,tas ../datasets/tas_sample.nc without_tas.nc
```

=== Answers:
<answers-2>
+ #strong[Drop the only variable:] CDO will produce an error --- the
  output file would be empty (no variables).
+ #strong[Select multiple variables:]
  `cdo selname,tas,pr,psl input.nc output.nc`
+ #strong[`selname` vs `selcode`:]
  - `selname` selects by variable name (e.g., `tas`, `pr`)
  - `selcode` selects by GRIB code number (e.g., 167 = 2m temperature,
    172 = precipitation)

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

== Task 4: Rename Variables
<task-4-rename-variables>
=== Command:
<command-1>
```bash
cdo chname,tas,temperature ../datasets/tas_sample.nc renamed.nc
```

=== Answers:
<answers-3>
+ #strong[Does renaming change data values?] No --- it only modifies the
  variable name in the metadata.
+ #strong[Does it preserve all metadata attributes?] Yes --- all
  attributes (units, long\_name, etc.) are preserved. Only the variable
  name string is changed.
+ #strong[Rename multiple variables:]
  `cdo chname,tas,temp,pr,precip input.nc output.nc`

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

== Task 5: Compress at Different Levels
<task-5-compress-at-different-levels>
=== Commands:
<commands-2>
```bash
cdo -z zip_1 copy ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_zip1.nc
cdo -z zip_5 copy ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_zip5.nc
cdo -z zip_9 copy ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_zip9.nc
```

=== Typical size comparison (sample file \~50 MB uncompressed):
<typical-size-comparison-sample-file-50-mb-uncompressed>
#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 3,
    align: (auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Level], [File Size], [Ratio],),
    table.hline(),
    [none], [50 MB], [1.00],
    [zip\_1], [18 MB], [0.36],
    [zip\_5], [15 MB], [0.30],
    [zip\_9], [14 MB], [0.28],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

=== Answers:
<answers-4>
+ #strong[Size reduction:] Level 1 gives significant reduction; levels
  5--9 give diminishing returns.
+ #strong[Compression is lossless] --- decompressed data is
  bit-identical to the original.
+ #strong[Trade-off of level 9:] Slightly better compression but
  significantly slower read/write times. Level 4--6 is usually
  recommended for the best balance.
+ #strong[Other compression methods:] CDO supports `szip` (SZIP), `grib`
  (GRIB packing), and `shap` (bit-shaving). Check with
  `cdo --help | grep -i compress`.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

== Bonus Challenge
<bonus-challenge>
=== One-liner:
<one-liner>
```bash
cdo -z zip_6 -chname,tas,temperature -selyear,2000/2005 ../datasets/tas_sample.nc final_output.nc
```
